---
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/ui/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/ui/PageHeader.astro";
import { owner, pageTitle, site, sitePages } from "../config/site";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { tools } from "../lib/registry";
import { aboutNodes } from "../lib/seo/jsonld";

// The one list of crumbs, drawn on the page and written into its structured data (ADR 0039).
const breadcrumbs = [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "About" }];
const description =
  "About NetworksInsights: a free online tools platform that works in your browser, with no accounts and no sign-up.";

// Counted from the registry, so the page never states a number the site does not have.
const toolCount = tools.length;
const categoryCount = new Set(tools.map((tool) => tool.manifest.category)).size;
const [github, linkedin] = owner.profiles;
---

<Page
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  title={pageTitle("About")}
  description={description}
  nodes={aboutNodes({ path: sitePages.about.href, name: "About", description })}
>
  <div class="grid gap-4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />
    <PageHeader
      title="About"
      intro={`${site.name} is a free online tools platform: ${site.tagline.toLowerCase()} for everyday jobs, in your browser.`}
    />
  </div>

  <div class="ni-prose">
    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name}{" "}
      collects small, single-purpose tools in one place: converting, compressing, calculating and
      creating things without installing anything. The tools are grouped into categories, and the
      collection grows over time.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free.</strong> Every tool is free to use.</li>
      <li><strong>No sign-up.</strong> There are no accounts.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fast.</strong>
        Pages are plain, static and light, so they load quickly even on a slow connection.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How your files are processed</h2>
    <p>
      Every tool page says where that tool does its work, in its Quick facts. Today no tool sends
      your files or your text to a server: the work happens in your browser, on your device. Light
      tools run on the page itself. Heavier ones, such as the PDF, image, video and audio tools, run
      in a background worker inside your browser, so the page stays responsive while they work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some tools first download a larger file from this site: a PDF engine, an image decoder, an
      audio encoder, a text-recognition language file or an AI model. That is a download only;
      nothing you enter goes with it. Each AI model is checked against its fixed SHA-256 fingerprint
      before it is used, and is not used if it does not match.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site can be installed as an app, and it keeps a copy of the pages you open so that they
      work without a connection. It never keeps what you type or the files you open in a tool. The
      <a href={sitePages.privacy.href}>privacy page</a>
      has the details.
    </p>

    <h2>How the tools are built and tested</h2>
    <p>
      Every tool follows the same contract: one folder with its logic, its interface, its page text
      and its tests. The logic is plain code with no access to the network or to the page, so it can
      be tested on its own, and every tool has unit tests for it. Every tool also has an end-to-end
      test that uses it in a real browser, in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.
    </p>
    <p>
      No change reaches the site until automated checks pass: type checks, the tests, checks on each
      tool's page text and structure, limits on how much JavaScript a page may load, a Lighthouse
      speed test of the pages on a mobile profile, and checks on the site's search data, security
      headers and the licences of the code it uses. The site is deployed only by those checks, never
      from a personal computer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each tool page states the tool's limits. Official figures, such as tax rates and bands, come
      from a data file that names the official source, the tax year and the date it was read, and
      the page shows all three.
    </p>

    <h2>Where things stand</h2>
    <p>
      The site went live in October 2026. It has {toolCount} tools in {categoryCount} categories,
      all free and none needing an account. New tools are added over time, and each one gets its own
      page that says what it does, how to use it, what its limits are and where it does its work.
    </p>

    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name}{" "}
      is built and run by {owner.name}, founder and developer, an individual in India. It is not a
      registered company. Wasey holds a B.Tech in Computer Science and Engineering (2025).
    </p>
    <p>
      Find Wasey on <a href={github.url}>{github.label}</a> and
      <a href={linkedin.url}>{linkedin.label}</a>. To write to the site, see the
      <a href={sitePages.contact.href}>contact page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Open-source credits</h2>
    <p>
      Some tools run open-source code in your browser. The projects, their licences and their
      notices are listed in full on the <a href={sitePages.credits.href}>credits page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Page>
